{"text": "Some authors believe. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That collectives of individuals can be more intelligent. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Than individuals taken singly. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Sometimes pointing to the example of ants, bees, and other social animals. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> It is certainly true. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That, by addressing a question to a collective crowd intelligence. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> You may get a good answer more easily. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Than by, say, researching the literature. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Or asking a philosopher. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But this works in a reliable fashion. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Only where some genuine expert happens to be in the crowd. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> This is because human intelligence is an individual capability. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That is non additive. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Collectives can, certainly, carry out plans that individuals cannot. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> ― such as building Cologne Cathedral. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Collectives can also evolve new social patterns. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Over very long periods of time. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Through trial and error. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Or through the sort of transgenerational collaboration. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That is involved, for example. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In the creation and sustainment of scientific disciplines. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But collectives do not write poetry or novels. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Discover new aspects of nature. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Or solve difficult mathematical problems. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> This is done predominantly. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> By individuals or by very small groups.", "alignment": {"characters": ["S", "o", "m", "e", " ", "a", "u", "t", "h", "o", "r", "s", " ", "b", "e", "l", "i", "e", "v", "e", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "=", "\"", "0", ".", "8", "s", "\"", " ", "/", ">", " ", "T", "h", "a", "t", " ", "